archive_core.py reuses the native archive writer and verifier from the KH1
Switch v2 workflow. egs_reader.py adapts the public archive key-expansion and
compression logic from AntonioDePau/KHPCPatchManager:
https://github.com/AntonioDePau/KHPCPatchManager
Upstream references: OpenKH/egsEncryption.cs, OpenKH/egsHDAsset.cs, OpenKH/hed.cs.
The upstream project credits Noxalus, Xeeynamo, and the OpenKH team.
Its Apache-2.0 license is included as LICENSE.

The intended external patch is TieuLink's German voices mod for KH2 Final Mix:
https://www.nexusmods.com/kingdomhearts2finalmix/mods/42
Credits on that mod page: TieuLink (patch and cutscene voices), RubyX_Coded
(Sora, Donald, Goofy and Roxas battle quotes), Fotia (bosses).
The external mod assets are NOT included. This package grants no rights in
those assets and does not rehost them. It reads a locally selected download.

movie_import.py is original workflow code. It invokes a separately installed
FFmpeg/ffprobe for local media inspection, conversion and decoding checks.
No FFmpeg executables or libraries are redistributed by this package.
The movie tests generate their own synthetic video and audio at runtime.

native_patch.py and scd_tools.py add native archive bindings and original-SCD
support. File-format references: OpenKh SCD documentation and vgmstream's
sqex_scd.c. The SCD/Ogg parsing is original Python workflow code.
Real user-provided audio samples were used privately for validation; no such
audio or native archive payloads are included. The film regression fixture
contains format metadata only. The HD regression fixture contains source
filename/size/CRC metadata and native resource names, without media payload.

scd_audio.py invokes the user's FFmpeg to match native Vorbis/MS-ADPCM codecs
when needed, retains cue/info tables, and validates converted audio by decoding.
Version 6 added eight explicitly confirmed HD bindings and SCD v4/MS-ADPCM support.
Version 7 added matching native channel counts, including the supplied mono
Scar voice to native quad Vorbis, and collects per-file preflight failures.
Version 8 separates PCM decoding and encoding, uses standard 1024-byte ADPCM
blocks, normalizes WAVE headers before validation, and retains external audio
process diagnostics. FFmpeg's encoder source documents the default block size:
https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg/blob/master/libavcodec/adpcmenc.c

Version 9 explicitly selects FFmpeg's libvorbis decoder, labels and reorders
Vorbis channels before remixing, and retries a crashed audio operation once
in a fresh process with FFmpeg's CPU flags disabled. Ordinary media failures,
timeouts and persistent crashes still stop output publication. Successful
recoveries remain in the diagnostic logs. Channel order references:
https://ffmpeg.org/doxygen/trunk/libvorbisdec_8c_source.html
https://xiph.org/vorbis/doc/Vorbis_I_spec.html
